## EMBEDDED NORMATIVE CONTENT (verbatim from the design docs; self-contained — do not consult the source markdown)

### CALM closed class vocabulary (CALM_AND_OBLIGATIONS §1) — exactly seven, checked in registries/calm_operations.tsv

- `monotone_with_authentication`: grow-only info whose union cannot invalidate earlier results, admitted only after identity/authorization verification;
- `monotone_scoped`: grow-only within one locally authorized scope (e.g. a verified cache); discardable without correctness loss;
- `commutative_but_bounded`: algebraically mergeable state with declared bounds, overflow behavior, and reset/regime semantics; inherently retractable observations (availability) belong here, never in a monotone class;
- `local_deterministic`: pure deterministic computation over pinned inputs; ordering/advice, never shared truth;
- `ordered_projection`: publication through a subordinate monotone anti-rollback authority (e.g. generation activation);
- `head_cas_required`: meaning depends on absence, replacement, uniqueness, or revocation; only the repository authority head can decide it;
- `exclusive_external_effect`: one externally observable side effect owned by an outbox obligation with stable idempotency.
An operation in an undefined class is a registry defect.

### The obligation trait and lifecycle (CALM §6)

Lifecycle: `Reserved -> Committed -> Acknowledged`, or `Reserved -> Aborted`. Commit makes the effect canonically owned; acknowledgement separately records external observation; a committed obligation outlives its region only as an explicit unacknowledged-effect record, never silent drop.

```rust
trait Obligation {
    type CommitReceipt;
    type AbortReceipt;
    type AckEvidence;
    fn commit(self, receipt: Self::CommitReceipt) -> CommittedObligation<Self::AckEvidence>;
    fn abort(self, receipt: Self::AbortReceipt) -> SettledObligation;
}
trait Acknowledge {
    type AckEvidence;
    fn acknowledge(self, evidence: Self::AckEvidence) -> SettledObligation;
}
```
Public obligation and committed-obligation types are `#[must_use]`. Dropping a reserved obligation without transfer/commit/abort, or a `CommittedObligation` without acknowledgement or an explicit unacknowledged-effect record, is a correctness failure in lab/test and a typed containment event in production.

### The eleven concrete obligation types (CALM §7)

ObjectAdmissionPermit; PreparedTxnSlot; HeadCasAttempt; OutboxEffectPermit; SecretLease; WorkspaceLease; RunnerSlot; RetentionPin; RepairPermit; ContextBudgetPermit; BillingReservation. Graded resource algebra dimensions: bytes; objects/pieces; CPU time; memory; file descriptors/sockets; network egress; money/quota; secret exposure duration; failure-domain slots; human approval capacity. Combining composes grades; splitting requires a conservation proof.

### CALM registry examples table (CALM §10) — operation | class | authority

Put verified Git object | monotone_with_authentication | object admission capability;
Add repair symbol | monotone_with_authentication | repair capability;
Cache verified pack | monotone_scoped | cache grant;
Add review comment event | head_cas_required | repository authority head;
Move branch ref | head_cas_required | repository authority head;
Activate search generation | ordered_projection | generation authority;
Remove legal hold | head_cas_required | repository authority head;
Deliver webhook | exclusive_external_effect | outbox obligation;
Add telemetry observation | commutative_but_bounded | telemetry profile;
Merge peer availability map | commutative_but_bounded | availability profile;
Rank Context candidates | local_deterministic | context planner.

### Conflict-absorbing lattice (CALM §5)

```text
          Conflict
          /      \
   Committed    Refused
          \      /
          Reserved
              |
           Unknown
```
Joining `Committed` and `Refused` yields sticky `Conflict` (blocks service until authority consulted). Timestamp choice cannot erase contradictory terminal facts. The canonical repository head is NOT a CRDT.

